	•	Section 1 Comments/Quotes: The study used de-identified United Network for Organ Sharing Standard Transplant Analysis and Research File data, described as SRTR/OPTN data, from US lung transplant recipients. Transplants before 8 November 2022 were included, and the abstract describes the period as 1987–2022. The authors stated, “This research received no funding” and declared no competing interests.

Section 2: Population (PICO - P)
	•	Target Population: Transplant Recipients (Post-op)
	•	Total Sample Size (N): 43869
	•	Overall Mean Age: NR
	•	Female Sex (%): NR
	•	Race/Ethnicity Reported: Yes
	•	Comorbidities / Clinical History Included: Yes
	•	Section 2 Comments/Quotes: The abstract reports 43,869 first-time lung transplant recipients, although the Results also describe an exploratory cohort of 47,864 recipients and Figure 1 reports 37,369 observations in the all-years modeling dataset. The study was restricted to first-time recipients older than 18 years. Overall mean age and female percentage were not reported. Recipient and donor ethnicity, transplant indication, functional status, infection, mechanical ventilation, ECMO, renal function, hemodynamics, lung function, and other clinical-history variables were included.

Section 3: Intervention & AI Methods
	•	Primary ML Component: Yes
	•	Study Design: Retrospective Cohort
	•	AI Model Architecture: Transformer/LLM
	•	Algorithm Name: EHRFormer
	•	Input Variables (Data Modality): Tabular (EMR/Clinical data)
	•	Comparator / Standard of Care: Other
	•	Validation Method: Internal Split (Train/Test)
	•	Explainability / Interpretability Used: Yes (e.g., SHAP, LIME)
	•	Feature Selection Method: Automated (e.g., LASSO, Stepwise)
	•	Hyperparameter Tuning Reported: Yes
	•	Section 3 Comments/Quotes: EHRFormer was a tabular BERT model with three transformer layers, 64-dimensional embeddings, an intermediate layer size of 128, and four attention heads. It was compared with XGBoost. Features were automatically filtered to those with less than 10% missingness within each era, producing 140–152 variables. The models used an 80:20 train/test split, five-fold cross-validation within training, and held-out test evaluation. EHRFormer hyperparameters were optimized with Optuna, while XGBoost used Bayesian optimization. Interpretability included SHAP for XGBoost and single- or multivariable in silico perturbations for EHRFormer.

